meta {
  name: Create Note With Attachment
  type: http
  seq: 8
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/notes
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{accessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "entityType": "TRUCK",
    "entityId": {{truckId}},
    "categoryId": {{categoryId}},
    "content": "Tyre bill attached",
    "attachments": [
      {
        "key": "{{attachmentKey}}",
        "fileName": "sample-attachment.txt",
        "contentType": "text/plain",
        "size": 59
      }
    ]
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.body && res.body.data && res.body.data.id) {
    bru.setEnvVar("noteId", res.body.data.id);
  }
}

docs {
  Step 2: add a note with the file from "Upload Attachment" (`attachmentKey`). Same endpoint and rules as
  "Create Note"; replies can carry `attachments` too.

  Each attachment needs `key` and `fileName`; `contentType` and `size` are optional and stored as sent.
  The key must start with `notes/<torgId>/` (the caller's torg) and the file must exist in storage. Trucks
  allow at most 10 files per note. Allowed types, by extension in any case (`.JPG` is fine): images (jpg,
  jpeg, png, gif, webp, bmp, tif, tiff, heic, heif), videos (mp4, mov, m4v, 3gp, avi, mkv, webm, hevc),
  pdf, documents (doc, docx, odt, rtf, txt, ppt, pptx, odp) and spreadsheets (xls, xlsx, xlsm, csv, ods).
  Both `fileName` and the key must have one of these extensions.

  The response note has `attachments: [{id, key, fileName, contentType, size, url}]`, where `url` is a
  short-lived signed link.
}
